---
const inputs = [
  { name: "Static analysis", body: "Syntax trees, symbol resolution, imports" },
  { name: "Runtime traces", body: "From test environments and production" },
  { name: "Declared by people", body: "Contracts, owners, risk labels, a few rules" },
];
const facts = [
  "Components and their boundaries",
  "Contracts: APIs, events, schemas, invariants",
  "Edges: calls, data flows, events, outside services",
  "Owners and risk labels",
  "Which tests cover which paths",
];
const consumers = [
  { name: "Reviewers", body: "Semantic change reports" },
  { name: "Permissions", body: "Scopes named in terms of the map" },
  { name: "Risk routing", body: "Blast radius and sensitivity" },
  { name: "Agents", body: "Questions over MCP and the CLI" },
];
const questions = [
  "What depends on UserPreferences?",
  "Which tests exercise the refund path?",
  "Who owns billing, and what are its invariants?",
  "If I change this function's return type, what breaks?",
  "Is there already a helper that formats phone numbers?",
];
---

<section class="section wrap map" aria-labelledby="map-title">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="map-title">One map, derived from code.</h2>
  <p class="prose lead">
    Hand-written architecture docs rot after the third refactor. Onus computes a live map of the codebase
    on every change, like a build artifact. People declare only a thin layer on top: contracts, owners,
    risk labels, and a few rules such as “billing may not reach into the internals of notifications.”
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="col col-in">
      <p class="col-label">Built from</p>
      {
        inputs.map((i) => (
          <div class="node">
            <p class="node-name">{i.name}</p>
            <p class="node-body">{i.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <div class="col col-map">
      <div class="map-node">
        <p class="map-name">The map</p>
        <ul role="list">
          {facts.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="map-foot">Rebuilt on every change</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="col col-out">
      <p class="col-label">Used by</p>
      {
        consumers.map((c) => (
          <div class="node">
            <p class="node-name">{c.name}</p>
            <p class="node-body">{c.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <figcaption class="caption">
      Static analysis misses runtime wiring, so the map combines what the code says could happen with
      what traces show did happen. Parts of the map with low confidence count as higher risk.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="ask">
    <p class="subhead">Agents ask the map instead of reading 50 files</p>
    <ul class="questions" role="list">
      {questions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="prose muted">
      The answers come back exact and cheap, which saves context, money and invented structure. When a
      proposed change breaks a declared rule, the map says so right away, before any judge or person is
      involved.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .lead {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .diagram {
    --gap: clamp(1.5rem, 0.5rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 56rem) {
    .diagram {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.15fr 1fr;
      column-gap: var(--gap);
      align-items: center;
    }

    .diagram figcaption {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }

    /* Connectors: inputs point into the map, the map points out to consumers. */
    .col-in .node::after,
    .col-out .node::before {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      top: 50%;
      width: var(--gap);
      height: 1px;
      background: var(--ink-2);
    }

    .col-in .node::after {
      left: 100%;
    }

    .col-out .node::before {
      right: 100%;
    }

    .col-in .node::before,
    .col-out .node::after {
      content: "";
      position: absolute;
      top: calc(50% - 4px);
      border: 4px solid transparent;
    }

    .col-in .node::before {
      left: calc(100% + var(--gap) - 7px);
      border-left: 7px solid var(--ink-2);
      border-right-width: 0;
    }

    .col-out .node::after {
      left: -7px;
      border-left: 7px solid var(--ink-2);
      border-right-width: 0;
    }
  }

  .col {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .col-label {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .node {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-paper);
    border-radius: var(--radius-strip);
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
  }

  .node-name {
    font-weight: 650;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .node-body {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .map-node {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
    border-radius: var(--radius-panel);
    padding: clamp(1.2rem, 0.8rem + 1.4vw, 1.8rem);
  }

  .map-name {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-stretch: 120%;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .map-node ul {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.9875rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .map-node li {
    padding-left: 1rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .map-node li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.55em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    background: var(--cobalt-soft);
    border-radius: 1px;
  }

  .map-foot {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 25%, transparent);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .ask {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 3vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .questions {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .questions li {
    background: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-paper);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.95rem;
    font-size: 0.9875rem;
  }
</style>
